{"subjectId":"python","id":"operators","title":"Operators & Precedence","group":"Getting Started","order":4,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Every operator Python has, how tightly each one binds, and the surprising corners — floor division with negatives, <code>and</code>/<code>or</code> returning operands, chained comparisons and bit tricks.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list-numbers\"></i>Precedence table (highest first)</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Operators</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td><code>(…)</code> <code>[…]</code> <code>{…}</code>, <code>x[i]</code>, <code>x(…)</code>, <code>x.attr</code></td><td>Grouping, subscription, calls, attribute access</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td><code>await x</code></td><td>Inside <code>async def</code></td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td><code>**</code></td><td>Power — <strong>right</strong>-associative; binds tighter than a unary minus on its left</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td><code>+x</code> <code>-x</code> <code>~x</code></td><td>Unary plus, minus, bitwise NOT</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td><code>*</code> <code>@</code> <code>/</code> <code>//</code> <code>%</code></td><td>Multiplicative (<code>@</code> is matrix multiplication)</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td><code>+</code> <code>-</code></td><td>Additive</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td><code>&lt;&lt;</code> <code>&gt;&gt;</code></td><td>Shifts</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td><code>&amp;</code></td><td>Bitwise AND</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td><code>^</code></td><td>Bitwise XOR</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td><code>|</code></td><td>Bitwise OR</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td><code>in</code> <code>not in</code> <code>is</code> <code>is not</code> <code>&lt;</code> <code>&lt;=</code> <code>&gt;</code> <code>&gt;=</code> <code>!=</code> <code>==</code></td><td>All comparisons share one level and <strong>chain</strong></td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td><code>not x</code></td><td>Boolean NOT</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td><code>and</code></td><td>Short-circuit AND</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td><code>or</code></td><td>Short-circuit OR</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td><code>x if c else y</code></td><td>Conditional expression</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td><code>lambda</code></td><td>Anonymous function</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td><code>:=</code></td><td>Assignment expression (walrus) — lowest</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Everything is left-to-right associative except <code>**</code> (right-to-left) and comparisons (which chain). When in doubt, add parentheses — they cost nothing and help the reader.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-calculator\"></i>Arithmetic</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>print(7 / 2, 7 // 2, 7 % 2)           # 3.5 3 1\nprint(-7 // 2, -7 % 2)                # -4 1\nprint(7 // -2, 7 % -2)                # -4 -1\nprint(divmod(-7, 2))                  # (-4, 1)\nprint(7.5 // 2, 7.5 % 2)              # 3.0 1.5\nprint(2 ** 3 ** 2, (2 ** 3) ** 2)     # 512 64\nprint(-2 ** 2, (-2) ** 2)             # -4 4\nprint(2 ** -1, 10 ** 20)              # 0.5 100000000000000000000</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Operator</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Watch out</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>/</code></td><td>True division</td><td>Always returns a <code>float</code>: <code>4 / 2 == 2.0</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>//</code></td><td>Floor division</td><td>Rounds toward <strong>−∞</strong>, not toward 0: <code>-7 // 2 == -4</code> (C/Java give <code>-3</code>)</td></tr><tr><td><code>%</code></td><td>Modulo</td><td>Result has the sign of the <strong>divisor</strong>: <code>-7 % 2 == 1</code>; always <code>(a // b) * b + a % b == a</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>**</code></td><td>Power</td><td>Right-associative; <code>pow(a, b, m)</code> does fast modular exponentiation</td></tr><tr><td><code>divmod(a, b)</code></td><td><code>(a // b, a % b)</code> at once</td><td>Handy for digit extraction and time conversions</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Python's <code>%</code> makes wrap-around indexing easy: <code>(i - 1) % n</code> is always a valid index, even when <code>i == 0</code>. For C-style truncation use <code>int(a / b)</code> or <code>math.fmod</code>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-equal\"></i>Comparisons chain</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>x = 5\nprint(1 &lt; x &lt; 10, 10 &gt; x &gt;= 5)        # True True\nprint(1 &lt; 2 == 2)                     # True   ← means (1 &lt; 2) and (2 == 2)\nprint((1 &lt; 2) == 2)                   # False  ← True == 2 is False\nprint(False == False in [False])      # True   ← (False == False) and (False in [False])\nprint(\"apple\" &lt; \"banana\" &lt; \"cherry\")  # True   ← strings compare lexicographically\nprint([1, 2] &lt; [1, 3], (2, 0) &gt; (1, 9))   # True True</code></pre></figure>\n<p><code>a &lt; b &lt; c</code> evaluates <code>b</code> only once and means <code>a &lt; b and b &lt; c</code>. Sequences compare element by element, which is why tuples make great multi-key sort keys.</p>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-logic-and\"></i><code>and</code>, <code>or</code>, <code>not</code> — short-circuit and return operands</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>print(0 or \"default\", \"x\" or \"default\")   # default x\nprint(0 and 1 / 0, 3 and 4)               # 0 4   ← 1 / 0 is never evaluated\nprint([] or None or 0)                    # 0     ← every operand falsy → the last one\nprint(not \"\", not \"hi\")                   # True False\n\nname = \"\"\nprint(name or \"Anonymous\")                # Anonymous</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Expression</th><th>Returns</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>a or b</code></td><td><code>a</code> if <code>a</code> is truthy, otherwise <code>b</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>a and b</code></td><td><code>a</code> if <code>a</code> is falsy, otherwise <code>b</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>not a</code></td><td>Always a real <code>bool</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p><code>count = user_count or 10</code> replaces a legitimate <code>0</code> with <code>10</code>. When <code>0</code>, <code>''</code> or <code>[]</code> are valid values, test explicitly: <code>10 if user_count is None else user_count</code>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-binary\"></i>Bitwise operators &amp; tricks</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>a, b = 0b1100, 0b1010                 # 12, 10\nprint(a &amp; b, a | b, a ^ b)            # 8 14 6\nprint(bin(a &amp; b), ~a)                 # 0b1000 -13   ← ~x == -x - 1\nprint(1 &lt;&lt; 4, 100 &gt;&gt; 2)               # 16 25        ← x &lt;&lt; k == x * 2**k</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def is_power_of_two(x):\n    return x &gt; 0 and x &amp; (x - 1) == 0     # clears the lowest set bit\n\nprint(is_power_of_two(64), is_power_of_two(96))   # True False\nprint(40 &amp; -40)                         # 8      ← lowest set bit of 0b101000\nprint((13 &gt;&gt; 2) &amp; 1)                    # 1      ← bit 2 of 0b1101\nprint((255).bit_count(), (255).bit_length())   # 8 8\n\nREAD, WRITE, EXEC = 1, 2, 4             # flags packed in one int\nperms = READ | WRITE\nperms &amp;= ~WRITE                         # clear a flag\nperms |= EXEC                           # set a flag\nprint(perms, bool(perms &amp; READ), bool(perms &amp; WRITE))   # 5 True False\n\nnums = [4, 1, 2, 1, 2]\nsingle = 0\nfor n in nums:\n    single ^= n                         # x ^ x == 0, so pairs cancel out\nprint(single)                           # 4</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Unlike C, bitwise operators bind <strong>tighter</strong> than comparisons in Python: <code>n &amp; 1 == 0</code> means <code>(n &amp; 1) == 0</code>. Python ints are unbounded, so <code>~</code> and negative numbers behave as if they had infinitely many leading 1 bits.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-plus\"></i>Augmented assignment</h3>\n<p><code>x += 1</code>, <code>-=</code>, <code>*=</code>, <code>/=</code>, <code>//=</code>, <code>%=</code>, <code>**=</code>, <code>&amp;=</code>, <code>|=</code>, <code>^=</code>, <code>&lt;&lt;=</code>, <code>&gt;&gt;=</code>, <code>@=</code>. For mutable objects they modify <strong>in place</strong> (<code>__iadd__</code>); for immutable ones they build a new object and rebind the name.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>a = [1]\nb = a\na += [2]              # list.__iadd__ → extends the same list\nprint(b)              # [1, 2]\na = a + [3]           # + builds a NEW list; b still sees the old one\nprint(a, b)           # [1, 2, 3] [1, 2]</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>There is no <code>++</code> or <code>--</code>. <code>i++</code> is a syntax error and <code>++i</code> quietly means <code>+(+i)</code> — it does nothing.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-split\"></i>Conditional expression &amp; walrus</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>age = 20\nprint(\"adult\" if age &gt;= 18 else \"minor\")          # adult\n\ndata = [4, 9, 1, 7]\nif (n := len(data)) &gt; 3:                          # assign and test in one step\n    print(f\"{n} items\")                           # 4 items\nbig_squares = [y for x in data if (y := x * x) &gt; 10]\nprint(big_squares)                                # [16, 81, 49]</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-fingerprint\"></i>Membership, identity &amp; overloading</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>print(\"ell\" in \"hello\", 3 in {1, 2, 3}, \"a\" in {\"a\": 1})   # True True True\na, b = [], []\nprint(a == b, a is b, a is not b)                         # True False True\nprint(\"ab\" * 3, [0] * 3, [1] + [2])                       # ababab [0, 0, 0] [1, 2]\n\nimport operator\nprint(operator.add(2, 3), operator.mul(\"ha\", 2))          # 5 haha</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Operators are method calls in disguise: <code>a + b</code> → <code>a.__add__(b)</code>, <code>a &lt; b</code> → <code>a.__lt__(b)</code>, <code>x in c</code> → <code>c.__contains__(x)</code>. That's how <code>str</code>, <code>list</code> and your own classes give <code>+</code> and <code>*</code> their own meaning (see <strong>Magic (Dunder) Methods</strong>). The <code>operator</code> module exposes each one as a function for <code>sorted(key=…)</code>, <code>reduce</code> and <code>map</code>.</p>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-alert-triangle\"></i>Common pitfalls</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>x = 5\nprint(x == 1 or 2)            # 2      ← parsed as (x == 1) or 2 → always truthy!\nprint(x in (1, 2))            # False  ← what was meant\nprint(not x == 5, x != 5)     # False False   ← not binds looser than ==\nprint(-3 ** 2)                # -9     ← -(3 ** 2)\nprint(0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3)       # False  ← floats; compare with math.isclose\nprint(1 + 2 * 3, (1 + 2) * 3) # 7 9</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What do and and or return?</summary><div><p>They return one of their <strong>operands</strong>, not necessarily a <code>bool</code>. <code>a or b</code> returns <code>a</code> if it's truthy, else <code>b</code>; <code>a and b</code> returns <code>a</code> if it's falsy, else <code>b</code>. Evaluation stops as soon as the result is known (short-circuit), so the right side may never run.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why is -7 // 2 equal to -4 in Python?</summary><div><p><code>//</code> is <strong>floor</strong> division: it rounds toward negative infinity. −3.5 floors to −4. Correspondingly <code>-7 % 2 == 1</code>, keeping the identity <code>(a // b) * b + a % b == a</code>. C and Java truncate toward zero instead (−3 and −1).</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How do you check whether a number is a power of two?</summary><div><p><code>n &gt; 0 and n &amp; (n - 1) == 0</code>. Subtracting 1 flips the lowest set bit and everything below it, so ANDing clears that bit — the result is 0 only if there was exactly one set bit.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What does 1 &lt; 2 == 2 evaluate to?</summary><div><p><code>True</code>. Comparisons chain: it means <code>1 &lt; 2 and 2 == 2</code>. It is <em>not</em> <code>(1 &lt; 2) == 2</code>, which would be <code>True == 2</code> → <code>False</code>.</p></div></details></section>"}